@node CMacs AI Providers
@section Providers

Eight providers are wired through the same @code{cmacs-ai-client-new}
DEFUN:

@table @code
@item claude
Anthropic Claude (HTTP).  Reads @env{ANTHROPIC_API_KEY} or
@env{CLAUDE_API_KEY}.
@item openai
OpenAI GPT (HTTP).  Reads @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}.
@item gemini
Google Gemini (HTTP).  Reads @env{GEMINI_API_KEY}.
@item grok
xAI Grok (HTTP).  Reads @env{XAI_API_KEY} or @env{GROK_API_KEY}.
@item ollama
Ollama (local HTTP).  Reads @env{OLLAMA_HOST}.
@item claude-code
The @command{claude} CLI in @option{--print} mode.
@item opencode
The @command{opencode} CLI.
@item claude-tmux
@command{claude} driven via an ephemeral tmux session.
@end table

@subsection A local model through the claude CLI

@code{claude-code} and @code{claude-tmux} treat a model beginning with
@samp{ollama/} as a request to use Ollama as the @emph{transport}:
ai-glib spawns

@example
ollama launch claude --model NAME -- @var{the claude arguments}
@end example

rather than @command{claude} directly, omitting claude's own
@option{--model} because Ollama supplies it.  Answer the model prompt
with @samp{ollama/qwen3.5:9b} after choosing @code{claude-code}, or write
the pair as one string wherever a @samp{PROVIDER/MODEL} is taken:

@example
claude-code/ollama/qwen3.5:9b
@end example

This is a different thing from the @code{ollama} provider, which talks
HTTP to the Ollama server with no CLI in the picture.  Use the CLI form
when you want that harness around a model that never leaves the machine;
use the plain @code{ollama} provider when you just want the model.

The claude CLI must still be installed, and @env{OLLAMA_PATH} overrides
the launcher binary.  @samp{ai -p claude-code -m ollama/NAME --dry-run
"hi"} prints the exact command without running it.

@node CMacs AI Chat Buffers
@section Chat Buffers

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-chat} opens a fresh chat buffer in
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-mode} (derived from @code{org-mode}).  The
buffer layout mirrors @code{cmacs-libreclaw-room-mode}:

@example
#+TITLE: cmacs-ai -- 2026-05-25 18:00:00
#+PROPERTY: provider claude

* Conversation
** 18:00:00  user
What is GLib?
** 18:00:00  assistant
GLib is a low-level C utility library...
* Compose                                              :compose:
@end example

History above the @code{* Compose} sentinel is read-only; the
region below is the editable prompt area.  @kbd{C-c C-c} sends the
compose body and streams the response into a fresh @code{** assistant}
heading via the @code{AiStreamable} signal pipeline.

@subsection One heading per exchange

What you send produces exactly one @code{** TIMESTAMP provider/model}
heading, however many times the model stops to call a tool.  Tool calls
and results nest under it as @code{***} entries, and what the model says
afterwards continues beneath.

The heading is opened by the model's first @emph{text}, not by the
stream.  A segment that only calls a tool has nothing to show, and
opening its heading up front is what littered a transcript with empty
ones---a bootstrap reading six files produced seven headings, most
blank.

Two consequences for code written against this:
@code{cmacs-ai-chat--assistant-marker} is nil in the gaps between the
streams of a tool loop, so it means ``no segment is appending'', not
``the chat is idle''; @code{cmacs-ai-chat--turn-open} is the flag that
spans the whole exchange, and anything asking whether the chat is busy
has to read that one or it will deliver into the middle of a turn.

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Send the compose region.
@item C-c C-k
Cancel an in-flight request.
@item C-c C-l
Clear conversation history (server-side and visible).
@item C-c C-s
Archive the chat history to @code{cmacs-ai-chat-dir} (the editable
@code{* Compose} region is excluded).  Always writes, regardless of
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-autosave}.
@item C-c C-o
Resume a saved chat (@code{cmacs-ai-chat-resume}): reopen its
transcript and rebuild the ai-glib session so it continues with full
context.
@end table

@subsection Saving and Resuming

Chats are archived automatically, mirroring how libreclaw persists
its rooms.  When @code{cmacs-ai-chat-autosave} is non-nil (the
default), a chat is written to @code{cmacs-ai-chat-dir} at three
points: right after you send a message, after each assistant response
completes, and when the buffer is killed --- so a conversation
survives a crash, a cancel, or an accidental close.  The archive
holds the conversation history (preamble plus the turns above
@code{* Compose}); the editable compose region is excluded.  The file
name comes from @code{cmacs-ai-chat-save-name-format} (default
@code{%y%m%d-%H%M%S-<provider>.org}, e.g.@:
@file{260605-142345-claude.org}).

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-resume-chat} (or @kbd{C-c C-o} from a chat buffer)
reopens an archived chat: it restores the transcript @emph{and}
rebuilds the ai-glib session by replaying the visible turns, so the
model keeps full prior context and further messages append to the
same file.  Reopening an already-resumed chat just switches to it.

Two things are intentionally not restored: tool-call / tool-result
blocks are replayed as plain text turns only (the rebuilt session
carries the conversation, not the verbatim tool I/O --- this avoids
provider errors from an orphaned @code{tool_result}); and each chat
is its own archive file (there is no per-project single-file model
like libreclaw's cmacs channel).

@node CMacs AI Region Commands
@section Region Commands

Acting on the active region without opening a chat buffer:

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-ai-rewrite-region
Prompt for an instruction; replace the region with the AI rewrite.
The current major mode is conveyed so the output uses the right
syntax.  Wrapped in one undo step.
@item M-x cmacs-ai-explain-region
Pop a side window with an Org-mode explanation of the region.
@item M-x cmacs-ai-doc-region
Insert an AI-written doc comment above the region (idiom matches
the language: docstring for Python/Elisp, @code{/** */} for
JavaDoc/Doxygen, etc.).
@item M-x cmacs-ai-test-region
Synthesize a unit test in a scratch buffer.
@end table

A right-click context menu entry is installed via
@code{cmacs-ai-context-menu-install}.

@node CMacs AI Generic Calls
@section Generic Calls

For scripting and user configs, @code{cmacs-ai-call} makes a one-shot
AI call from Elisp and returns the answer as a string -- with or
without tools.  Gate on @code{IS-CMACS-AI} if the config must also run
on a build without @option{--with-cmacs-ai}.

@example
;; Plain call, returns a string.
(cmacs-ai-call "Summarise this in one line: ...")
;; Agentic call with the built-in tools; the final answer is returned.
(cmacs-ai-call "What files are in my home directory?" :builtin-tools t)
@end example

@code{cmacs-ai-call} takes a plist: @code{:provider} (symbol),
@code{:system}, @code{:model}, @code{:max-tokens}, @code{:tools} (tool
specs), @code{:builtin-tools}, @code{:mcp-bridge} (needs
@option{--with-cmacs-mcp}), @code{:search-provider}, and
@code{:directory} (@pxref{CMacs AI Tool Use}).  Any tool
option builds a throwaway executor, uses it, and frees it.  It signals
@code{cmacs-ai-error} on failure.

@code{cmacs-ai-define-tool} builds a tool spec whose Elisp callback's
@emph{return value} is handed back to the model.  The callback is
@code{(funcall CALLBACK NAME INPUT-JSON ID)} and must return a string
(nil signals a tool error).  The executor runs synchronously on the
main thread, so callbacks may safely touch Emacs state.  Under the hood
@code{cmacs-ai-call} wraps the C primitive @code{cmacs-ai--call}; the
no-tools path equals @code{cmacs-ai-prompt-sync}.

@subsection From C, cmacsgi, D-Bus, emacsctl, and MCP

The calls are DEFUNs, so C and any eval-bridge client can drive them
(@code{cmacsgi eval '(cmacs-ai-call "...")'} works unchanged).  Typed
verbs also exist: @code{emacsctl ai prompt} and @code{emacsctl ai call}
(with @option{-t/--tools} for the built-in agent tools); the D-Bus
methods @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Ai.Prompt (ssss)} and
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Ai.Call (ssssb)}; and the MCP tools
@code{ai_prompt} and @code{ai_call}.  External callers cannot register
Elisp tools, so their tool-enabled call uses the server-side built-in
executor.

@quotation Warning
A tool-enabled call runs a synchronous multi-turn loop (capped at ~20
turns) on the target's main thread.  Under @command{emacs --gowl} that
thread is the Wayland compositor -- a long loop freezes the desktop.
Keep external tool loops short, and prefer @code{ai prompt} (no tools)
when driving a live @option{--gowl} session.
@end quotation

@node CMacs AI Inline Completion
@section Inline Ghost-Text Completion

@code{cmacs-ai-completion-mode} is a minor mode that drives idle-
timer-based fill-in-the-middle completion.  After
@code{cmacs-ai-completion-idle-delay} seconds of no input, a request
is fired with the buffer prefix + suffix as FIM context; the result
renders as a @code{shadow}-faced after-string overlay.  @kbd{TAB}
accepts; any other key dismisses.

Default provider is @code{claude-code} (CLI wrapper) so per-keystroke
HTTP costs are avoided; override with
@code{cmacs-ai-completion-provider}.

@node CMacs AI Voice Loop
@section Voice Loop

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-voice-chat} opens a chat buffer wired for full
voice round-trip: whisper transcribes a held-down recording via
@code{cmacs-whisper-dictate}, ai-glib streams the reply, and
@code{cmacs-piper-speak-region} speaks each completed sentence as
it streams in.  All four subsystems (@code{cmacs-whisper},
@code{cmacs-piper}, @code{cmacs-audio}, @code{cmacs-ai}) must be
enabled.

@table @code
@item cmacs-ai-voice-auto-speak
Whether replies are spoken.  Default @code{t}.
@end table

@node CMacs AI Coding Agent
@section Coding Agent

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-agent-open} opens (or resumes) the agent chat
for the current project root.  Before the first turn, it builds a
project-aware system prompt:

@itemize
@item
The first matching file from @code{cmacs-ai-agent-project-files}
(default: CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, README.org, README.md) is read in
full and attached as ``Project agent contract''.
@item
The absolute project root path is included.
@end itemize

This makes the assistant honor the project's own conventions
without manual cut-and-paste.

@node CMacs AI Tool Use
@section Tool Use

@code{cmacs-ai-tools-new} returns an @code{AiToolExecutor} handle
with all of ai-glib's built-ins pre-registered:

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{bash} -- run a shell command
@item @code{read} / @code{write} / @code{edit} -- file I/O
@item @code{glob} / @code{grep} / @code{ls} -- file discovery
@item @code{web_fetch} -- fetch a URL (HTML converted to text;
optional @code{prompt} extraction)
@end itemize

Per the project default, all tools are enabled (full trust).
Set @code{cmacs-ai-tool-confirm} to @code{'destructive} for a
prompt before bash/write/edit, or to a function for per-call
custom approval.

@code{web_fetch} is model-aware: HTML responses are converted to
readable markdown-style text (scripts and styles stripped, headings
and links preserved; images surface as Markdown @code{![alt](URL)}
with the src resolved to an absolute URL, so the model can cite or
embed real image links), @code{http://} is upgraded to @code{https://},
a @code{User-Agent} and a 30-second timeout are applied, and converted
bodies are cached in-process for 15 minutes.  With an optional
@code{prompt} argument, a sub-model reads the fetched page (through the
chat's active provider) and returns only the requested information
instead of the whole page.

@subsection Where the tools run

The built-in tools resolve relative paths against the executor's working
directory, and @code{bash} runs its command there.

@defun cmacs-ai-tools-set-working-directory executor directory
Point @var{executor}'s built-in tools at @var{directory}.  @code{nil}
restores the default, which is the Emacs process's own working
directory---for a long-lived editor, wherever you last visited a file,
and almost never what the model meant by @file{src/main.c}.  An absolute
path from the model is always used as given.

@code{cmacs-ai-call} takes the same thing as @code{:directory}.
Underneath it is ai-glib's
@code{ai_tool_executor_set_working_directory}; @code{bash} spawns
through @code{GSubprocess} rather than @code{popen} so the directory can
be set at all, which also makes the command cancellable and keeps its
output valid UTF-8.
@end defun

@subsection Custom Elisp tools

Custom Elisp tools are registered via
@code{cmacs-ai-tools-register}; the callback is invoked with
@code{(NAME INPUT-JSON ID)} on each model call and must return a string.

Callbacks may safely touch buffers and Emacs state.  On the synchronous
@code{cmacs-ai-call} path they run on the main thread directly; on the
background path (@code{cmacs-ai-tools-run-async}, which is what an
in-process brigade agent uses) the tool loop runs on a worker thread, so
the call is marshalled to the main thread and waited for.  It has to be:
Emacs's allocator is not thread-safe, so every @code{Lisp_Object}
involved must be built where Lisp lives, and only C strings cross the
boundary.

The run's own messages---the assistant's turns and the results of any
tools it called---are appended back onto the session before the callback
fires.  That is what lets the same session be run again as a
continuation rather than a restart: without it ai-glib's executor copies
the message list, appends to its copy and frees it, so a second run would
show the model its original instruction and the new question with no
trace of what it had already said or done.

Because every turn re-sends the whole session, a long conversation costs
quadratically in the number of turns, and does it quietly.

@defopt cmacs-ai-history-limit
Most messages a session keeps before the oldest are dropped (200).  The
oldest end is both the part the model is least likely to still need and
the only part safe to lose: standing instructions live in the client's
system prompt, not in this list, so they are never what gets dropped.
Zero or negative keeps everything, which is the right setting only if you
are watching the cost yourself.
@end defopt

@code{cmacs-ai-session-append-message} takes only @code{user} and
@code{assistant}.  There is no @code{system} role: a system prompt
belongs to the client, and is set with
@code{cmacs-ai-client-set-system-prompt}.

@defopt cmacs-ai-tool-call-timeout
Seconds a background tool loop waits for the main thread (120).  The
main thread picks the call up from its GLib dispatch, which happens
whenever Emacs reaches the event loop, so in practice this is immediate;
if it is not, the agent gets a timeout error for that one tool call
rather than the worker parking for the rest of the session.  Raise it if
you have Elisp tools that legitimately take minutes.
@end defopt

In addition to ai-glib's built-ins, every chat buffer is augmented
with cmacs's own MCP tool surface (buffer / file / project / eval /
apropos / describe by default).  See @ref{CMacs AI MCP Bridge}.

@subsection Web Search

@code{web_search} returns ranked results (title, URL, source domain,
snippet) and can optionally fetch the top results' pages.  Unlike
@code{web_fetch} it is not a free built-in: it is registered on a
chat/agent buffer's executor only when a backend is configured.
@code{cmacs-ai-chat--init} does this automatically from
@code{cmacs-ai-search-provider} (default @code{'auto}):

@table @code
@item auto
Brave or Bing when its key is set, else keyless DuckDuckGo.
@item brave
Brave Search (needs a key).
@item bing
Bing Search (needs a key).
@item duckduckgo
Keyless DuckDuckGo (best-effort, no SLA).
@item nil
Disabled.
@end table

Keyed backends read @code{cmacs-ai-search-api-key}, falling back to the
@code{BRAVE_API_KEY} / @code{BING_API_KEY} environment variable.  A keyed
provider with no key available logs a message and leaves @code{web_search}
off; @code{auto} always succeeds thanks to the keyless fallback.

Once registered, the model may use the tool's options: @code{count},
@code{freshness} (any/day/week/month/year), @code{safesearch}
(off/moderate/strict), @code{country}, @code{language}, @code{site}, and
@code{fetch_content} (fetch and attach the top results' page text via the
@code{web_fetch} pipeline).  Results are cached in-process for five
minutes.  Switch the backend for an open chat at runtime with
@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-chat-set-search-provider}; inspect the active tool set
with @code{(cmacs-ai-tools-list @var{executor})}.

@subsection Inline Images

When @code{cmacs-ai-chat-inline-images} is non-nil (the default), image
links in an assistant response render inline after each turn.  Have the
model embed images as bare Org links, e.g.@:
@code{[[https://host/pic.png]]} (a described link @code{[[url][text]]}
shows the text, not the image).  Local/file images preview synchronously
via Org; remote @code{http(s)} images are fetched @emph{asynchronously}
and shown when they arrive, so a slow or dead URL never blocks Emacs
(Org's own remote-image download is synchronous and would freeze the UI).
Non-image responses, error statuses, and undecodable data are ignored
silently.  Width is capped at @code{cmacs-ai-chat-image-max-width} (or
~92% of the chat window); @kbd{C-c C-x C-v} (@code{org-link-preview})
clears the previews.

@node CMacs AI Image Generation
@section Image Generation

Generate an image and put it where you are working.  In an Org buffer the
image is attached to the current node, linked, and previewed inline; the
same machinery is reachable from an @code{ai-image} babel block, from the
chat buffer, and over MCP / D-Bus / @command{emacsctl}.

